{"abstract":"Computer vision is crucial for ensuring a safe future for the quickly growing underwater infrastructure monitoring industry, which currently relies primarily on labour-intensive and costly manual methods. Over the last twelve years, deep learning models have revolutionized the field of computer vision and have been applied across various domains. These models could potentially assist in developing underwater surveys, for example, by analyzing videos and image data from equipment inspection and environment monitoring, thereby automating the process and reducing the time spent on visual inspections. Despite the success of deep learning models, underwater images impose challenges not faced with in-air images. Underwater images are generally of poor quality with issues such as blur, haziness, non-uniform illumination, and color degradation. These factors raise concerns about the performance of well-known deep learning models in underwater applications. As a subset of machine learning, deep learning models are datadriven, and the quality of the training data impacts the model’s performance. Furthermore, as a consequence of the large number of parameters, they are data-greedy, requiring large datasets for effective training. However, large underwater datasets are difficult to generate and not widely available. Collecting underwater data relies on the availability of underwater vehicles, specialists to perform the survey, and favorable weather and water conditions. Once collected, the data needs to be labeled. Data annotation is an extremely laborious task, prone to human errors, which reduces the quality of the final dataset. Furthermore, a well-known problem in deep learning is the overconfidence of the models, which can predict outputs with high probability even for inputs out of distribution (OOD) of the training data. This dissertation leverages this overconfidence by employing predictive uncertainty to identify the most important images for labelling, addressing limited financial or human resources for data annotation. An extensive review of the state-of-the-art of deep learning models applied to underwater images concluded that predictive uncertainty is rarely used in this field. The literature review also revealed the scarcity of large underwater datasets and the researchers’ efforts to develop models and training strategies to overcome this limitation. This lack of publicly available datasets is even more pronounced for industrial applications. To address this gap, this thesis supported the development and release of three publicly available underwater RGB datasets: MIMIR (synthetic), SubPipe, and MarinaPipe (real-world datasets). The contribution for the MIMIR project consisted of evaluating the dataset usability in the context of pipeline segmentation. In the SubPipe and MarinaPipe projects, the work consisted on annotating and evaluating the datasets for image segmentation. MarinaPipe was recorded in very shallow waters, resulting in images with visible sunlight rays, and contains pipelines occluded by the sediments from the marina floor. SubPipe was recorded in deeper waters, leading to darker images, with many parts of the pipeline covered by sand. The links for downloading these three datasets are available in the REMARO GitHub (https://github.com/remaro-network). The overconfidence in deep learning models is an overwhelming concern. However, it is possible to leverage this overconfidence by calculating the predictive uncertainty to assess the models’ lack of knowledge and use this information to reduce the effort required to generate labeled datasets. This thesis investigated the hypotheses that, given a model trained on synthetic data, the predictive uncertainty enables the selection of real-world images about which the model demonstrates little to no knowledge, for fine-tuning the model and bridging the synthetic-to-reality gap that exists even for photorealistic images. Selecting images based on uncertainty, calculated with Monte Carlo dropout, resulted in a model with better performance compared to randomly selecting the same amount of images. Additionally, this research explored using predictive uncertainty calculated with Monte Carlo dropout for active learning in the underwater domain. When training with real underwater pipeline images and using uncertainty-driven active learning for selecting images, at least 15.9% fewer annotations were needed to achieve the same performance as a model trained on randomly selected images. In addition, this PhD research trained a generalized few-shot segmentation model and used predictive uncertainty to evaluate the reliability of the predictions using mutual information and entropy. The experiments performed indicate that predictive uncertainty both increases the reliability of deep learning models and optimizes the use of human and financial resources available for data annotation by selecting the most informative data samples.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Marnet, Luiza Ribeiro"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808921"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"vision based classification for underwater safety critical applications","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109527263/ITU_Copenhagen_Thesis_Template_1_black_links-compressed.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808921","ids":["itu-b0460769-2268-408e-b7ff-6e7726d1fe33"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-64E2BE09DE30BD3B63093FF0D2D0AA2C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109527263/ITU_Copenhagen_Thesis_Template_1_black_links-compressed.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luiza Ribeiro","key":"marnetpau","last":"Marnet","locid":"0562500c-e9cb-43cf-9641-8775d47103cc","name":"Marnet, Luiza Ribeiro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779495364"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"238","pages":"186","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b0460769-2268-408e-b7ff-6e7726d1fe33"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Vision-based classification for underwater safety critical applications","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808921","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Active learning aims to select the minimum amount of data to train a model that performs similarly to a model trained with the entire dataset. We study the potential of active learning for image segmentation in underwater infrastructure inspection tasks, where large amounts of data are typically collected. The pipeline inspection images are usually semantically repetitive but with great variations in quality. We use mutual information as the acquisition function, calculated using Monte Carlo dropout. HyperSeg is trained using active learning with an underwater pipeline inspection dataset of over 50,000 images. To allow reproducibility and assess the framework’s effectiveness, the CamVid dataset was also utilized. For the pipeline dataset, HyperSeg with active learning achieved 67.5% meanIoU using 12.5% of the data, and 61.4 % with the same amount of randomly selected images. This shows that using active learning for segmentation models in underwater inspection tasks can lower the cost significantly.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Ribeiro Marnet, Luiza","Grasshof, Stella","Brodskiy, Yuri","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730182"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Active learning","Computer vision","Underwater inspection"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["EIVA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"uncertainty driven active learning for image segmentation in underwater inspection","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.14002.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-730182","ids":["itu-950ebbcc-5a10-4ece-919f-f1beb600add2"],"keyword":["Active learning","Computer vision","Underwater inspection"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-15FA40FC63DF05822113463C38168637"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.14002.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14002","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["EIVA"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Audio-Visual Computing","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luiza","key":"ribeiromarnetpau","last":"Ribeiro Marnet","name":"Ribeiro Marnet, Luiza","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stella","key":"graßhofpau","last":"Graßhof","locid":"ad50bed7-77fd-4c7f-819c-54455506e3f0","name":"Grasshof, Stella","name_org":"Graßhof, Stella","orcid":"0000-0002-6791-7425","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuri","key":"brodskiypau","last":"Brodskiy","name":"Brodskiy, Yuri","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-950ebbcc-5a10-4ece-919f-f1beb600add2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Uncertainty Driven Active Learning for Image Segmentation in Underwater Inspection","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730182","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Labeling images for every new task or data pattern a model needs to learn is a significant time bottleneck in real-world applications. Moreover, acquiring the necessary data for training the models can be challenging. Ideally, one would train the models with simulated images and adapt them for the desired real tasks using the least possible amount of data. Active learning can be used to solve this problem with minimal effort. In this work, we train SegFormer for pipeline segmentation with synthetic images from an underwater simulated environment and fine-tune the model with real underwater pipeline images recorded in a marina. The evaluation shows that selecting real data with active learning for fine-tuning the model gives better results than randomly selecting the images. As part of the work, we release the dataset recorded in the marina, MarinaPipe, which will be publicly available.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Ribeiro Marnet, Luiza","Grasshof, Stella","Brodskiy, Yuri","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764506"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Active learning","Computer vision","Image segmentation","Underwater inspection"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["EIVA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bridging the sim to real gap for underwater image segmentation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"funding_acknowledgement":["funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie grant agreement No. 956200"],"id":"S01-000.00-764506","identifier":[{"id":"85206447240","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4be18a27-833b-4475-944f-6314d0445bcf"],"keyword":["Underwater inspection","active learning","computer vision","image segmentation","sim-to-real"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FB6E8E748F7A58E12007FA6000723D75"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["EIVA"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Audio-Visual Computing","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luiza","key":"ribeiromarnetpau","last":"Ribeiro Marnet","name":"Ribeiro Marnet, Luiza","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stella","key":"graßhofpau","last":"Graßhof","locid":"ad50bed7-77fd-4c7f-819c-54455506e3f0","name":"Grasshof, Stella","name_org":"Graßhof, Stella","orcid":"0000-0002-6791-7425","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuri","key":"brodskiypau","last":"Brodskiy","name":"Brodskiy, Yuri","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-4be18a27-833b-4475-944f-6314d0445bcf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Bridging the Sim-to-Real GAP for Underwater Image Segmentation","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764506","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents MIMIR-UW, a multipurpose underwater synthetic dataset for SLAM, depth estimation, and object segmentation to bridge the gap between theory and application in underwater environments. MIMIR-UW integrates three camera sensors, inertial measurements, and ground truth for robot pose, image depth, and object segmentation. The underwater robot is deployed within a pipe exploration scenario, carrying artificial lights that create uneven lighting, in addition to natural artefacts such as reflections from natural light and backscattering effects. Four environments totalling eleven tracks are provided, with various difficulties regarding light conditions or dynamic elements. Two metrics for dataset evaluation are proposed, allowing MIMIR-UW to be compared with other datasets. State-of-art methods on SLAM, segmentation and depth estimation are deployed and benchmarked on MIMIR-UW. Moreover, the dataset's potential for sim-to-real transfer is demonstrated by leveraging the segmentation and depth estimation models trained on MIMIR-UW in a real pipeline inspection scenario. To the best of the authors' knowledge, this is the first underwater dataset targeted for such a variety of methods. The dataset is publicly available online. https://github.com/remaro-network/MIMIR-UW/","author_count":7,"contributors":["Alvarez Tunon, Olaya","Kanner, Hemanth","Ribeiro Marnet, Luiza","Pham, Xuan Huy","Sejersen, Jonas le Fevre","Brodskiy, Yury","Kayacan, Erdal"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-717318"],"event":{"end":"2023-10-01","place":"Detroit, us","start":"2023-10-01","title":"2023 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS)","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Estimation","Inspection","Measurement","Object Segmentation","Pipelines","Robot vision systems","Simultaneous localization and mapping"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mimir uw a multipurpose synthetic dataset for underwater navigation and inspection","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85174993207","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie grant agreement No 956200"],"id":"S01-000.00-717318","identifier":[{"id":"85174993207","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-235dee56-574b-4df9-b1e6-d92f884b8d1c"],"keyword":["Measurement","Simultaneous localization and mapping","estimation","inspection","object segmentation","pipelines","robot vision systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C4EEC36387550FC8F3496871D53D2C8C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering - Signal Processing and Machine learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["SMV company (Denmark)"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olaya","key":"alvareztunonpau","last":"Alvarez Tunon","locid":"0ba2ed83-69f9-48ec-b11b-89da6